  ],
  "difficulty": 3,
  "marks": 6,
  "estimatedSeconds": 540,
  "stem": "The continuous random variable $X$ has pdf $f(x) = k(4 - x^2)$ for $0 \\le x \\le 2$, and $f(x) = 0$ otherwise.",
  "parts": [
   {
    "label": "(a)",
    "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
    "marks": 2,
    "markingMode": "MANUAL",
    "stem": "Show that $k = \\frac{3}{16}$.",
    "answerSpec": {
     "markScheme": {
      "points": [
       {
        "id": "p1",
        "description": "M1 sets $\\int_0^2 k(4 - x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x = 1$ and integrates: $k\\left[4x - \\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^2$.",
        "marks": 1
       },
       {
        "id": "p2",
        "description": "A1 $k \\times \\frac{16}{3} = 1$, so $k = \\frac{3}{16}$.",
        "marks": 1
       }
      ]
     },
     "modelAnswer": "$$\n\\int_0^2 k(4 - x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x = k\\left[4x - \\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^2 = k\\left(8 - \\frac{8}{3}\\right) = \\frac{16k}{3} = 1\n$$\nso $k = \\frac{3}{16}$."
    },
    "explanation": "Total area 1."
   },
   {
    "label": "(b)",
    "type": "NUMERICAL",
    "marks": 2,
    "stem": "Find E(X).",
    "answerSpec": {
     "value": 0.7499999999999959,
     "tolerance": {
      "kind": "absolute",
      "amount": 0.0005
     }
    },
    "explanation": "$\\frac{3}{16}\\int_0^2 (4x - x^3)\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\frac{3}{16}(8 - 4) = 0.75$.",
    "workedSolution": "1. $\\frac{3}{16}\\int_0^2 (4x - x^3)\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\frac{3}{16}(8 - 4) = 0.75$."
   },
   {
    "label": "(c)",
    "type": "NUMERICAL",
    "marks": 2,
    "stem": "Find P(X < 1), giving your answer as an exact fraction or a decimal.",
    "answerSpec": {
     "value": 0.6875000000000039,
     "tolerance": {
      "kind": "absolute",
      "amount": 0.0006
     }
    },
    "explanation": "$\\frac{3}{16}\\left[4x - \\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^1 = \\frac{3}{16} \\times \\frac{11}{3} = \\frac{11}{16} = 0.6875$.",
    "workedSolution": "1. $\\frac{3}{16}\\left[4x - \\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^1 = \\frac{3}{16} \\times \\frac{11}{3} = \\frac{11}{16} = 0.6875$."
   }
  ],
  "explanation": "Total area 1 gives k; E(X) = ∫x f(x) dx; probabilities are areas.",
